# Scenario 1 — Given: the installed component and a 32-byte fixture key
# When: each function is called with a valid signature
1	1	x
# Then: all three answer and the round trip returns the plaintext (above)
# Scenario 2 — Given: the same key
# When: gcm_encrypt is called with too few arguments
# Then: the call is rejected at init with the usage message (errors section)
# Scenario 3 — Given: the same key
# When: gcm_decrypt is called with too many arguments
# Then: the call is rejected at init with the usage message (errors section)
# Scenario 4 — Given: a key that is 5 bytes instead of 32
# When: gcm_encrypt is called with it
# Then: bad_key_len, reported with lengths only and no key bytes (errors section)
# Scenario 5 — Given: a key that is 31 bytes, one short of AES-256
# When: gcm_decrypt is called with it
# Then: bad_key_len — the length check runs on every call (errors section)
# Scenario 6 — Given: a 33-byte key, one byte too long
# When: gcm_encrypt_det is called with it
# Then: bad_key_len — no folding, hashing or padding to length (errors section)
